  "grammar": [
    {
      "title": "Verbs + Prepositions (Verbos + Preposiciones)",
      "content": "Many Spanish verbs require a specific preposition before the next verb or noun. Unlike English, where prepositions are often intuitive, Spanish verbs lock onto their prepositions rigidly.\n\nMost important A2.2 verb+preposition combinations:\n- pensar EN (to think about) — 'Pienso en mi futuro.' (I'm thinking about my future.)\n- salir CON (to go out with / to date) — 'Ella sale con su novio.' (She goes out with her boyfriend.)\n- empezar A (to start to) — 'Empieza a llover.' (It starts to rain.)\n- soñar CON/EN (to dream about) — 'Sueño con viajar a Japón.' (I dream of traveling to Japan.)\n- depender DE (to depend on) — 'Depende de ti.' (It depends on you.)\n- llamar A (to call) — 'Llamo a mi madre.' (I call my mother.)\n- acabar de (to have just) — 'Acabo de llegar.' (I just arrived.)\n- aprender A (to learn to) — 'Aprendo a cocinar.' (I'm learning to cook.)\n- ayudar A (to help) — 'Ayudo a mi madre.' (I help my mother.)\n- creer EN (to believe in) — 'Creo en la paz.' (I believe in peace.)\n- olvidarse DE (to forget about) — 'Se olvida de todo.' (He/She forgets about everything.)",
      "table": {
        "headers": [
          "Spanish Verb",
          "Preposition",
          "English",
          "Example"
        ],
        "rows": [
